ative of $f$ on $I$, which function is also an antiderivative of $f$?","options":["$CF$ for any constant $C\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$F+Cx$ for any constant $C\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$F+C$ for any constant $C\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$F(x+C)$ for any constant $C\\in\\mathbb{R}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"585c8504bb5219f304cfa4e7","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_antiderivative_4f8b183c_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which function is an antiderivative of $f(x) = \\cos x$?","options":["$F(x) = \\cos x+2$","$F(x) = -\\sin x$","$F(x) = x\\cos x$","$F(x) = \\sin x+2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6b4ba1bbc2ec92b19ef469ff","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_antiderivative_4f8b183c_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is the interval $I$ relevant for antiderivative?","options":["It fixes $C$ through the value of $F$ at the left endpoint","$F'(x)=f(x)$ needs to hold only at one point $x_0\\in I$","The equality $F'(x) = f(x)$ must hold for all $x\\in I$","It ensures that $F$ is the unique antiderivative on $I$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"43a02b146151a56cd3223451","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_antiderivative_4f8b183c_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement best reflects the family viewpoint for antiderivatives?","options":["All antiderivatives of a function are treated as differing by constants","An antiderivative is unique whenever $f$ is non-zero","All antiderivatives of a function differ by a constant positive factor","All antiderivatives of a function differ by a linear term $Cx$ with $C$ constant"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1ea54fb6327293c16858f32d","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_antiderivative_4f8b183c_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $F(x) = x^2$ is an antiderivative of $f(x) = 2x$, 